WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-nominated actor

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.204
you know from "Boardwalk Empire"
and films

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:06.772
like "Nocturnal Animals"
and "Revolutionary Road."

00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:08.841
He stars in
"Nine Perfect Strangers,"

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:10.276
which is streaming on Hulu

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.011
with new episodes
premiering Wednesdays.

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:13.379
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.148
-God. You got some balls,
shaming me about some wine

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:17.717
and a couple of KIT KATs.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.854
All the while, you're dosing us
with psychedelics?

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.722
I mean, look, I'm no angel.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:25.859
You know, I had a few
cocaine nights in the '90s,

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.827
but who didn't?
-I didn't!

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.028
-I believe that.

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:31.363
-You know, you --
You could go to jail for this.

00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:33.299
-I could, but I'm not going to,

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.334
because you're going
to be thanking me.

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:38.404
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Michael Shannon.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:41.608
[ Applause ]

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:42.775
-Hey, everybody.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.210
Thanks, bud.
-Thanks for being here, buddy.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.245
Always a pleasure.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:48.281
Hey.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.783 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.752
-It's so lovely to have you
back in the studio.

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.054
-Oh, it's nice to be here.
It's been a hot minute.

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:56.388
-It has been a hot minute
and a lot of --

00:00:56.388 --> 00:00:58.525
It's been a hot mess in between
the two hot minutes.

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:01.127
-Yes, a hot messy minute.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:04.029
So, you play
one of nine perfect strangers

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:05.531
who go to
a-a wellness retreat...

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:07.266
-Yes, sir.
-...run by Nicole Kidman.

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:09.034
You -- I mean,
you're a Brooklyn guy...

00:01:09.034 --> 00:01:10.370
-Uh-huh.
-...not an L.A. guy.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.971
I feel like wellness
is a real L.A. thing,

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.406
but I guess Brooklyn
a little bit, too.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.774
Have you ever bought into
the wellness craze?

00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:17.677
-Well, yeah, there's a --

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.779
There's a yoga place
down the block from my house,

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:23.917
and I-I tried it out.

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.918
-How'd it go?
-Uh...

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:29.321
Yeah, I don't have
the inherent, intrinsic balance,

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:30.689
I think, to pull it off.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:32.291
-Yeah.
-I got frustrated.

00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:35.761
They kept asking us
to do things on one foot

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:38.731
or, you know, put your foot
in your knee or do all these --

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:43.302
And I just would always kind of
wobble and eventually fall over.

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:48.240
So I wound up injuring myself
more than anything. So --

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.710
-I also -- Please do not
take this the wrong way,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.111
but I feel like I know you,

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:54.113
I know you to be
a very sweet person.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:55.915
But I feel like people
that know you from your work,

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.184
if they were, like,
in a yoga studio and saw you,

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.086
they would not --
It would be, like, not,

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.555
like, a good,
very peaceful thing.

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:03.923
-You know, honestly,

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:07.526
I-I don't think I intimidated
any of these people.

00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:08.928
I think they --

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:10.563
I think they felt sorry for me.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:11.731
[ Laughter ]

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:15.400
They thought, "You really
don't belong here."

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.371
But, you know, it's --

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:21.341
It's an amazing -- It's an
amazing kind of strength,

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.976
the people that can actually
do this stuff.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.511
It's not, you know, they're not
like bodybuilder strength,

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:28.514
but they -- They're really
strong, and I can't do it.

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:29.748
-Yeah, they're sneaky strong.

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:31.818
I think that, like,
if I could go back in time

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.786
and had started anything,
it would not be a sport.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.822
It would've been, like,
as a young man, do yoga.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.790
I feel like I'd be a lot --
I'd be in a lot better shape.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.192
-Yeah, yoga, pilates.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:41.561
-Yeah.
-Pilates.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.996
-We both -- We both
sort of came up in Chicago.

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:47.733
I feel like that would have been
even more frowned upon

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:49.168
amongst the Chicagoans.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:52.371
-Well, you know, it's funny, I
was just at this little theater.

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:55.108
I'm with the Red Orchid
in Chicago on Wells Street,

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:58.044
and right next door
to our theater,

00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:00.179
they opened a pilates studio.

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:01.581
-Yeah, see? It's changing.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.349
But I do think
like 20, 25 years ago,

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.384
it was maybe a little bit harder
to find pilates in Chicago.

00:03:05.384 --> 00:03:07.553
-It was. Yeah, it was more --

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:09.856
That whole neighborhood
has just really changed.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:12.992
There used to be like a Ripley's
Believe It or Not over there,

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.027
like, a head shop, and stuff.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:17.096
It was kind of like
the Haight-Ashbury of Chicago,

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:20.600
and now it's just
totally squared up.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.803
-It's really hard to keep a
Ripley's Believe It or Not open

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.004
these days.
-I know,

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.306
and now when we need them
more than ever.

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:28.608
-Yeah, we really --

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:30.142
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:31.344
-Can you believe it?

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:32.945
-Yeah.

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:33.980
"Go down to the Ripley's."

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.449
"I tried to. It's a pilates."

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:37.783
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:40.786
-You got to spend time
in Australia shooting this.

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:42.855
-I did.
-Did you enjoy that?

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.158
-It's beautiful. You know,
I had been there 20 years ago

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:47.626
to make "Kangaroo Jack."

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:50.062
But that was --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:51.496
-This is where you wish --

00:03:51.496 --> 00:03:53.066
Yeah, I mean, my God.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:54.967
[ Applause ]

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:58.438
I mean, I -- I've gotten used
to not having an audience.

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:01.407
But if there was ever a time
I wish we had one --

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:04.110
-Uh --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.512
No, I just -- I've actually --

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.680
Never mind.
So, but that was a very --

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.283
That was in the outback.
And this was on the beach,

00:04:11.283 --> 00:04:14.119
Byron Bay, which is

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:17.256
just one of the most exquisite
beaches in the world,

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.725
well regarded for surfing.
-Yeah.

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:22.461
-Everyone there is constantly
talking about surfing.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:25.297
"Are you surfing today?
Are you surfing later?

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:26.933
Are you surfing tomorrow?

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.368
Is it the right day
to go surfing?"

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.572
And I-I-I just was --
felt a little out of my element,

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:34.206
'cause I don't surf at all.

00:04:34.206 --> 00:04:36.943
-Any -- Was -- Did you have
any pull to try that?

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.079
-I tried
the paddleboarding one day.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.514
-Right, which is not surfing.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.950
-No. It seems like an easier
version of surfing.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:47.453
-Yes.
-But -- But very much like yoga,

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.822
I don't think
I had what it takes.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:52.191
-Yeah, that's true. If you
couldn't do "stand on one foot,"

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.259
I feel like you've
eliminated yourself.

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:56.529
-[ Laughing ] I can't
stand on a paddleboard.

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:59.298
So, but I --

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.800
I-I took long walks
on the beach,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.303
long, romantic
walks on the beach.

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:05.938
By myself.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:08.207
-Can they be romantic
if you're by yourself?

00:05:08.207 --> 00:05:11.510
-Well, depends on how you feel
about yourself, I guess.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:12.845
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.348
-Did you see --
I mean, I'm wondering,

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:16.482
you must be a celebrity

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.717
to some degree
amongst even the wildlife,

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:20.186
based on "Kangaroo Jack."

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:22.688
But did you see --
Did you see any --

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:24.623
I mean, like, do people
actually see koalas

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:25.824
when you go to Australia?
-Yes.

00:05:25.824 --> 00:05:27.693
It's very rare.
-It is.

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:30.595
-The koalas are having
a tough go of it right now.

00:05:30.595 --> 00:05:31.831
-Yes.
-And --

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:34.133
But we were -- As --
If you watch the show,

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:38.838
you'll see a kind of
a natural, outdoorsy place.

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:44.577
And -- And we actually saw two
of them throughout the shoot,

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.746
and, which everybody on the crew

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:51.684
told us how unbelievably lucky
we were to be seeing these.

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:53.418
And they're -- They're --
They're amazing.

00:05:53.418 --> 00:05:57.323
They really are, as much
as you've seen pictures of them

00:05:57.323 --> 00:06:01.060
or, you know, cartoon,
animes, whatever,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.831
to actually see one in the flesh
is pretty remarkable, yeah.

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.399
-That's really cool.
I feel like that would --

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:08.768
That ticks the box
of the Australian experience.

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:10.570
-Yeah.
-You also have --

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.138
Your daughter
went to school there,

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:13.672
because you went
for the whole year, right?

00:06:13.672 --> 00:06:15.641
-Well, I went for five months,

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:19.077
and my family was able to
join me for about half of that.

00:06:19.077 --> 00:06:23.316
And my little daughter Marion
was able to go to school,

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:25.484
because where there --
where we were,

00:06:25.484 --> 00:06:29.055
there weren't any cases,
and it was pretty safe.

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:30.623
So once you did your quarantine,

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:33.425
you could kind of go about
a regular life, you know?

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:36.395
So, yeah, we got her
into this Catholic school,

00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:41.133
just 'cause it was kind of
the easiest one to get her into.

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:44.503
And, yeah, we're not
devout people,

00:06:44.503 --> 00:06:45.938
a devout family ourselves,

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:49.775
but she actually had
a little exposure to that.

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:51.243
-How'd she take to it?
Did she dig it?

00:06:51.243 --> 00:06:54.113
-She really did, because
it wasn't the kind of stern,

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:56.148
you know, nuns with rulers
kind of --

00:06:56.148 --> 00:06:58.717
Like, her teacher was named,
like, Miss Honeybell.

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:00.286
-Oh, my.
-And she's a beautiful blonde.

00:07:00.286 --> 00:07:03.856
She was just always smiling.
And they had very uplifting --

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:06.592
like, they had a little thing
they'd say at lunch

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:07.693
before they ate their lunch.

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:10.529
It was, "Yum, Yum, munch, munch,

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:12.331
thank you, Jesus,
for our lunch."

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:13.866
-Same.
-And it was like, "Yay!"

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:15.768
You know, it was more like --

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.470
-Yeah, it kind of, like,
makes you think,

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.071
"Why did they ever
go with nuns?"

00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:21.774
-Yeah, right? It was --

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:24.710
You know,
if I could turn back time --

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.311
-There's a lot of things
we would have done.

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:28.214
We'll be right back with more
from Michael Shannon.

